[center]
[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]Presets: Mod Author Guide[/b][/size][/color][/center]
[line]
A preset distributes locations as a plain text file. Users drop it in a folder and SLM handles loading, updating and conflict checks.

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]How it works[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*][b]Drop and load:[/b] users drop your [font=Courier New].txt[/font] in the folder, and it appears in-game.
[*][b]Auto-updates:[/b] change your location later and SLM updates it for the user.
[*][b]Conflict checks:[/b] SLM checks for existing locations, so your preset never spams the list or overwrites a location the user made themselves.
[/list]

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]1. Creating your Preset[/b][/size][/color]
[list=1]
[*][b]Go there:[/b] stand in your spot in-game.
[*][b]Save it:[/b] add it to SLM with a name, category and description.
[*][b]Export it:[/b][list]
[*][b]Single location:[/b] the copy button on the location row.
[*][b]Whole category:[/b] right-click a Category header, then [b]Export Category[/b].
[*][b]Whole district:[/b] right-click a District header, then [b]Export District[/b].
[*][b]A set you pick:[/b] [b]Settings[/b] tab, then [b]Export Selected[/b] - tick the locations you want.
[*][b]Everything:[/b] [b]Settings[/b] tab, then [b]Export All Data[/b].
[/list]
[*][b]Save to file:[/b][list]
[*]Create a text file, for example [font=Courier New]MyCustomLocation.txt[/font].
[*]Paste the exported code string into it.
[*]Keep it to [b]one preset per file[/b]. Five spots to share means five [font=Courier New].txt[/font] files, so users can install only the ones they want.
[/list]
[/list]

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]2. Packaging it up[/b][/size][/color]
Pack your mod so your files land in:

[code]bin/x64/plugins/cyber_engine_tweaks/mods/SimpleLocationManager/presets/[/code]

Name the file something unique, like [font=Courier New]AuthorName_LocationName.txt[/font], so it cannot clash with another author's preset.

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]3. Updating a preset later[/b][/size][/color]
SLM remembers the [b]ID[/b] inside your export string.

To move a location, update your [font=Courier New].txt[/font] with the new export string. When the user installs your update, SLM matches the ID and [b]updates the location in place[/b] - no duplicate markers in their list.

[b]One exception, and it is deliberate:[/b] if the user has edited that location themselves, SLM marks it as theirs and your update skips it. Their changes win.

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]4. Conflict detection[/b][/size][/color]
SLM checks for existing locations before importing anything new.

[list]
[*]If the user, or another mod, already has a location within [b]0.5 metres[/b] of yours, your import is [b]skipped[/b].
[*]That prevents duplicate waypoints stacking up, and means a preset can never overwrite a location the user created by hand.
[/list]

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]5. Uninstalling a preset[/b][/size][/color]
Removing your mod takes the [font=Courier New].txt[/font] away, but the locations it already imported stay in the user's list.

[b]Settings[/b] tab, then [b]Remove Preset Locations[/b], lists every preset whose file is gone and clears out what it left behind - one preset at a time, so other presets are untouched. Any location the user edited themselves is kept and becomes their own.

Worth mentioning on your own mod page, so users know the tidy-up exists.

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]6. Verification[/b][/size][/color]
The import lines are off by default. To see them: [b]Settings[/b] tab, then [b]Console Logging[/b], set to [b]Debug[/b].

Open the CET console and reload. You are looking for:

[list]
[*][font=Courier New]Imported Location: The Afterlife Roof[/font] - it is in.
[*][font=Courier New]Synced Preset Location: ...[/font] - an existing location was updated.
[*][font=Courier New][SKIP] Conflict: Position match...[/font] - skipped because something was already there, which is the system working as intended.
[*][font=Courier New][FIX] Resyncing ID for location: ...[/font] - a broken ID link was repaired.
[/list]

Set [b]Console Logging[/b] back to [b]Warn[/b] when you are done, or the console fills with one line per location on every load.
